doc Invariant: invariant cond stops the program, saying where and what, if cond is false.
  Use it for what must stay true, e.g. inside a loop:

  ```
  while lo < hi
    invariant lo >= 0 && hi <= len(xs)
    ...
  end
  ```

  If the condition is false, the program writes the position, the
  condition as written, and the message after it, to stderr, and exits
  with status 2:

  ```
  app.pkg:12:3: invariant failed: n >= 0 && n <= len(xs): n is out of range
  ```

  The message is any number of `str` expressions, written one after
  another, and evaluated only when the check fails: `require n > 0, "got ",
  show(n)` with `strfmt`. Swap `use check` for
  `use nocheck` to turn every check into nothing (the conditions are
  still type-checked).

needs std/if std/not std/exprstmt check/fail
syntax statement
  invariant {c: expr bool} [, {m: expr str}]*
expand
  if !{c}
    check_fail({@pos}, "invariant failed", {quote c})
    {each g in ,}
    check_note({g.m})
    {end}
    check_stop()
  end
